{
  "_comment": "Example `<workdir>/crustify/artifacts/modules.json` (REPO-ROOT tier), emitted by the planner. A module is one translation unit with the headers it defines its items in, named by the TU's path; a header that implements what no TU does is a header module, named by its own path, with a null `translation_unit`. Every file is an entry whose `items` lists are empty when the module owns the file whole, `included` when it owns only the entities named, and `excluded` when it owns all others: here the shared include/openssl/crypto.h is split item by item among the modules that implement what it declares, and crypto/o_str.c, which mixes string lifecycle routines with other code, is split into its foundational part and the module that keeps its name. Each link unit's and module's `index` is its permanent id, not its position: the order is computed from `depends_on`, whose `scheduled` dependencies run first and whose `cut` dependencies, cut from a cycle, run after; among ready modules a foundational one runs first, then the lower link-unit index, then the lower index. Here libcrypto runs before libssl, which depends on it, and statem's dependency on record is cut. `status` is `todo` while the plan may change and `frozen` once final. `objective` records translation intent, and its `rust_equivalent` which items the standard library already provides and the crates that could replace the module; each out-of-tree import's `rust_equivalent` names the crates that port the library to Rust and those that bind it safely. The waves are in `schedule.json`. The Rust tree mirrors this decomposition; there is no second placement document. All paths are workdir-relative.",
  "version": "1f053298e30c0a9b5d0f3c1e7a4b8d2f6c9e0a11",
